e0$ is the nondegeneracy actually used); the normal orientation conditions $k_i\cdot n>0$, $k_r\cdot n<0$, the two normal interface identities, and the optional normal-root selection rule were not used, and no outgoing or tangential-growth condition was used. If a consuming construction declares the reflected wave absent, $e_r=h_r=0$ (so that $A_r=0$ and only the incident and transmitted traces are matched), the same grouping argument applies to the two nonzero trace data $A_i$ and $-A_t$: a singleton group would give $\pm A_i=0$ or $\mp A_t=0$, impossible by step 1.1, so $\lambda_i=\lambda_t$, i.e. $\kappa_i=\kappa_t$ and $\omega_i=\omega_t$; no constraint on the labels $k_r,\omega_r$ of the absent field is produced, and a Fresnel construction may assign it the common phase as a convention. [step 1.1, step 4.1, A1, F1, algebra] 6.1 [Angles and laws, part (c)] Assume now that $k_i,k_r,k_t$ are real. For each $j$, [F1] gives $k_j\cdot k_j=|k_j|^2=\varepsilon_j\mu_j\omega^2$ with the common $\omega=\omega_j$ of step 4.1, so $|k_j|=\omega(\varepsilon_j\mu_j)^{1/2}=\omega/v_j>0$ by [F6] and [F1], and $|k_i|=|k_r|$. By [F3], $|k_j|^2=|\kappa_j|^2+(k_j\cdot n)^2$, so $|\kappa_j|\le|k_j|$ and the ratio $\sin	heta_j:=|\kappa_j|/|k_j|$ lies in $[0,1]$; define $	heta_j:=\arcsin(|\kappa_j|/|k_j|)\in[0,\pi/2]$ by [F5], so that $\sin	heta_j=|\kappa_j|/|k_j|$ as in the Statement. Since $\kappa_r=\kappa_i=\kappa_t=\kappa$ by step 4.1, $\sin	heta_r=|\kappa|/|k_r|=|\kappa|/|k_i|=\sin	heta_i$; the principal inverse sine is strictly increasing on its interval because sine is strictly increasing on $[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$ by [F5], hence injective, so $	heta_r=	heta_i$. Similarly $|\kappa|=\omega(\varepsilon_1\mu_1)^{1/2}\sin	heta_i=\omega(\varepsilon_2\mu_2)^{1/2}\sin	heta_t$, that is $n_1\sin	heta_i=n_2\sin	heta_t$ after multiplying by $c/\omega$ where $n_j=c(\varepsilon_j\mu_j)^{1/2}=c/v_j$ by [F1] and [F6]. If $	heta_i>0$ then $\sin	heta_i>0$ and division is legitimate, giving $\sin	heta_t/\sin	heta_i=(\varepsilon_1\mu_1)^{1/2}/(\varepsilon_2\mu_2)^{1/2}=v_2/v_1=n_1/n_2$; if $	heta_i=0$ then $|\kappa|=0$, so $|\kappa_t|=0$ and $	heta_t=\arcsin 0=0$, since $\sin 0=0$ and the principal inverse sine returns the unique number of $[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$ with sine $0$, without forming $0/0$. [step 4.1, step 5.1, F1, F3, F5, F6, algebra] 7.1 [Scope, part (e)] The four statements (a)-(d) are conditional on the given wave data and the interface hypothesis [A1]; nothing about the existence of interface-consistent data, reflection or transmission amplitudes, energy fluxes or well-posedness is proved. All frequencies are positive, all tangential quantities may be complex, and clauses (a) and (b) cover complex transmitted wavevectors; the propagating angle selection of (c) and the amplitudes are the business of the consuming interface theorems. The interface identities on the noncompact plane are the stated hypothesis; for compact interfaces the distributional jump theorem [[pthm-em-material-stationary-jumps]] supplies them, and no distributional or choice-theoretic clause of that theorem is used here (only complex arithmetic, the character lemma, and the real geometry of the propagating case), so no choice principle is inherited. [given, step 5.2, F2, F7] ∎
